# %% [markdown]
# # Sales Performance Analysis — Multimedia Shop
# **Goal:** find where profit comes from and where it is lost, then turn that into actions.
# **Dataset:** Sample Superstore (Kaggle), 2014–2017 orders.
# **Steps:** load → clean → select features → EDA → targeted analyses → insights → export for the website.

# %%
import json
from pathlib import Path
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

plt.rcParams.update({"figure.figsize": (11, 5), "axes.spines.top": False,
                     "axes.spines.right": False, "font.family": "monospace"})
COLORS = ["#E63946", "#F4C430", "#1E6FD9", "#111111"]
FIG = Path("figures"); FIG.mkdir(exist_ok=True)

# %% [markdown]
# ## 1. Load the data
# The file uses latin-1 encoding (special characters in product names).

# %%
df = pd.read_csv("../data/superstore.csv", encoding="latin-1")
print(df.shape)
df.head()

# %%
df.info()

# %% [markdown]
# ## 2. Data cleaning
# **Why:** wrong types, missing values or duplicate rows would distort every sum below.

# %%
# 2.1 Missing values
missing = df.isna().sum()
print("Missing values per column:\n", missing[missing > 0] if missing.any() else "None")
df = df.dropna(subset=["Sales", "Profit", "Order Date"])           # critical columns
if "Postal Code" in df: df["Postal Code"] = df["Postal Code"].fillna(0)

# 2.2 Duplicates
before = len(df)
df = df.drop_duplicates()
print(f"Duplicates removed: {before - len(df)}")

# 2.3 Types
df["Order Date"] = pd.to_datetime(df["Order Date"], errors="coerce")
df["Ship Date"]  = pd.to_datetime(df["Ship Date"], errors="coerce")
df = df.dropna(subset=["Order Date"])

# %% [markdown]
# ## 3. Feature selection
# IDs, customer names and postal codes carry no business signal for these questions.
# We keep the variables that answer **when / where / what / how much / at what discount**.

# %%
features = ["Order Date", "Segment", "Region", "Category", "Sub-Category",
            "Sales", "Quantity", "Discount", "Profit"]
data = df[features].copy()
data["Year"]   = data["Order Date"].dt.year
data["Month"]  = data["Order Date"].dt.to_period("M")
data["Margin"] = np.where(data["Sales"] > 0, data["Profit"] / data["Sales"], 0)
data.head()

# %% [markdown]
# ## 4. Exploratory Data Analysis

# %%
data[["Sales", "Quantity", "Discount", "Profit"]].describe().round(2)

# %%
total_sales, total_profit = data["Sales"].sum(), data["Profit"].sum()
margin = total_profit / total_sales * 100
loss_share = (data["Profit"] < 0).mean() * 100
print(f"Revenue: ${total_sales:,.0f} | Profit: ${total_profit:,.0f} | Margin: {margin:.1f}%")
print(f"Order lines sold at a loss: {loss_share:.1f}%")

# %%
fig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 2)
data["Sales"].clip(upper=data["Sales"].quantile(.95)).hist(bins=40, ax=ax[0], color=COLORS[2])
ax[0].set_title("Sales distribution (≤ 95th pct)")
data["Profit"].clip(data["Profit"].quantile(.02), data["Profit"].quantile(.98)).hist(bins=40, ax=ax[1], color=COLORS[0])
ax[1].set_title("Profit distribution (2–98th pct)")
plt.tight_layout(); plt.savefig(FIG / "distributions.png", dpi=150); plt.show()
# Reading: sales are right-skewed (many small orders, few big ones); profit has a long negative tail.

# %% [markdown]
# ## 5. Monthly sales trend

# %%
monthly = data.groupby("Month")[["Sales", "Profit"]].sum()
ax = monthly["Sales"].plot(color=COLORS[0], lw=2.5, marker="o", ms=3)
monthly["Sales"].rolling(3).mean().plot(ax=ax, color=COLORS[3], ls="--", label="3-month avg")
ax.set_title("Monthly sales"); ax.set_ylabel("$"); ax.legend(["Sales", "3-month avg"])
plt.tight_layout(); plt.savefig(FIG / "monthly_trend.png", dpi=150); plt.show()

season = data.groupby(data["Order Date"].dt.month)["Sales"].mean()
print("Strongest months:", season.nlargest(3).index.tolist())
# Reading: steady yearly growth + Q4 seasonality → plan stock and marketing for Sep–Dec.

# %% [markdown]
# ## 6. Regional performance

# %%
region = data.groupby("Region")[["Sales", "Profit"]].sum().sort_values("Sales", ascending=False)
region["Margin %"] = (region["Profit"] / region["Sales"] * 100).round(1)
region

# %%
x = np.arange(len(region)); w = .38
plt.bar(x - w/2, region["Sales"], w, label="Sales", color=COLORS[2])
plt.bar(x + w/2, region["Profit"], w, label="Profit", color=COLORS[0])
plt.xticks(x, region.index); plt.title("Sales & profit by region"); plt.legend()
plt.tight_layout(); plt.savefig(FIG / "region.png", dpi=150); plt.show()

# %% [markdown]
# ## 7. Category & sub-category performance

# %%
cat = data.groupby("Category")[["Sales", "Profit"]].sum().sort_values("Sales", ascending=False)
cat["Margin %"] = (cat["Profit"] / cat["Sales"] * 100).round(1)
cat

# %%
cat[["Sales", "Profit"]].plot(kind="barh", color=[COLORS[1], COLORS[0]])
plt.title("Sales vs profit by category"); plt.tight_layout()
plt.savefig(FIG / "category.png", dpi=150); plt.show()

# %%
sub = data.groupby("Sub-Category")["Profit"].sum().sort_values()
sub.plot(kind="barh", color=[COLORS[0] if v < 0 else COLORS[2] for v in sub], figsize=(9, 7))
plt.title("Profit by sub-category"); plt.axvline(0, color="k", lw=1)
plt.tight_layout(); plt.savefig(FIG / "subcategory.png", dpi=150); plt.show()
# Reading: Technology (phones, copiers) drives profit; Tables / Bookcases / Supplies lose money.

# %% [markdown]
# ## 8. Discount vs profit
# **Hypothesis:** discounts increase volume but erode profit beyond a threshold.

# %%
print("Correlation discount ↔ profit:", round(data["Discount"].corr(data["Profit"]), 3))

disc = data.groupby("Discount").agg(Profit=("Profit", "sum"), Orders=("Sales", "size"),
                                    AvgMargin=("Margin", "mean")).round(3)
disc

# %%
fig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 2)
ax[0].scatter(data["Discount"], data["Profit"], alpha=.25, s=10, color=COLORS[2])
ax[0].axhline(0, color="k", lw=1); ax[0].set_title("Each order line"); ax[0].set_xlabel("Discount")
ax[1].bar(disc.index.astype(str), disc["Profit"], color=[COLORS[2] if v >= 0 else COLORS[0] for v in disc["Profit"]])
ax[1].axhline(0, color="k", lw=1); ax[1].set_title("Total profit by discount level"); ax[1].tick_params(axis="x", rotation=45)
plt.tight_layout(); plt.savefig(FIG / "discount_profit.png", dpi=150); plt.show()
# Reading: profit is positive up to 20 % discount, negative above → cap discounts at 20 %.

# %% [markdown]
# ## 9. Business insights
# 1. **Discount policy:** above 20 %, total profit turns negative. Cap discounts at 20 %.
# 2. **Technology** has the highest sales *and* the best margin: prioritise stock and promotion.
# 3. **Furniture** sells a lot but keeps little: reprice or drop loss-making tables and bookcases.
# 4. **Seasonality:** Sep, Nov and Dec are the peaks. Plan inventory and campaigns ahead of time.
# 5. **Regions:** West and East lead. Central's weak margin is driven by heavy discounting.

# %% [markdown]
# ## 10. Export for the website

# %%
def bucket(d):  # round discounts to the nearest 10 %
    return f"{int(round(d * 10) * 10)}%"

disc_b = data.assign(B=data["Discount"].apply(bucket)).groupby("B")["Profit"].sum()
disc_b = disc_b.reindex(sorted(disc_b.index, key=lambda s: int(s[:-1])))

out = {
    "kpi": {"sales": round(total_sales), "profit": round(total_profit), "margin": round(margin, 1)},
    "monthly": {"labels": [str(p) for p in monthly.index], "sales": monthly["Sales"].round().tolist()},
    "region": {"labels": region.index.tolist(), "sales": region["Sales"].round().tolist(),
               "profit": region["Profit"].round().tolist()},
    "category": {"labels": cat.index.tolist(), "sales": cat["Sales"].round().tolist(),
                 "profit": cat["Profit"].round().tolist()},
    "discount": {"labels": disc_b.index.tolist(), "profit": disc_b.round().tolist()},
}
Path("../data/data.json").write_text(json.dumps(out, indent=2))
print("data.json written ✔")
